tel 1.0

base ethereal
import soundness

info   daemonized command-line applications: one warm JVM serving every invocation through a native launcher
detail
    _Ethereal_ turns a command-line application into a **daemon**: a JVM which starts on the
    first invocation and stays resident, so that every later invocation is dispatched to it by
    a small native **launcher** and returns at native-tool speed, with the JVM's startup cost
    and its just-in-time compiler's warm-up paid once rather than on every run. The
    application's code is the same as a plain CLI's: the body of an `application` block,
    wrapped in `cli` instead.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
    import executives.completionsExecutive
    import interpreters.posixInterpreter
    import threads.virtualThreads

    @main
    def mytool(): Unit = cli:
      execute:
        Out.println(t"Hello world")
        Exit.Ok
    ```

    What differs is the process model. A plain `application` is one process per invocation,
    and its arguments, environment, working directory, standard streams and signals are the
    process's own. Under `cli`, one process — the daemon — serves many invocations at once,
    and *none* of those things is global: each invocation arrives on its own connection, with
    its own arguments, its own `Environment`, its own working directory, its own stdin, stdout
    and stderr, its own exit status and its own signals, all forwarded by the launcher and
    all read through the same `arguments`, `Out`, `Err`, `Exit`, `trap` and `Environment` as a
    plain application uses. Scala's own `println` writes to the daemon's `System.out`, which
    reaches no user: output goes through `Out` and `Err`, which resolve the invocation's
    `Stdio`, and a library that writes `System.out` is redirected with `Stdio.divert`, which
    follows the thread and the tasks spawned under it rather than the JVM.

    The launcher is the Rust runner published by `propensive/xek`
    (`curl -fsSL https://propensive.dev/xek | sh` installs it), and `xek build mytool.jar`
    joins the platform's stub, a configuration record and the application JAR into one
    executable. An invocation of that executable is one connection to the daemon over a
    Unix domain socket, carrying BinTEL documents of the `ethereal-launcher` TEL schema
    (`Launcher.schemaText`, the resource `ethereal/ethereal-launcher.tel`): an `init`
    document with the invocation's arguments, environment, working directory, user, terminal
    flags and descriptors, then `data` chunks of every stream under per-stream credit,
    `signal`/`signal-ack`, `mode`, `run`/`exited` and `closed`, until the daemon's
    `exit-status` ends the session. Every document carries the schema's 33-byte signature, so
    a launcher and a daemon built against different contracts refuse each other at the first
    document rather than misread each other; `Launcher.Message` is the enum which mirrors the
    schema, and `Launcher.encode`/`Launcher.decode` its codec.

    The **daemon** is started by the launcher with a sanitized environment of its own, in `/`,
    under umask `077`, so that nothing about the daemon's state depends on who happened to
    start it: code which reads `environments.javaBaseEnvironment` or
    `workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory` sees that state, not the invocation's. The
    invocation's own environment and working directory are the givens
    `environments.daemonClientEnvironment` and `workingDirectories.daemonClientWorkingDirectory`
    (imported by name — `import soundness.*` imports no givens), and the environment the
    daemon was itself launched with is `DaemonEnvironment`, the given
    `environments.daemonEnvironment`. The daemon keeps its state under
    `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/<name>/` (or the state home): a `socket`, a `pid` file, a `build` file
    recording the launcher it was started from and an `acceptance` file naming the schema it
    speaks; it logs `DaemonLogEvent`s to syslog under `ethereal`, retires itself after six
    idle hours, when a state file is removed, when its launcher is rebuilt, or when asked, and
    the built-in `'{admin}'` subcommand reports its pid (`pid`), ends it (`kill`) or drains it
    (`shutdown`: no further invocation is served, those in flight finish, then it exits). Set
    `ETHEREAL_DEBUG=1` in the shell to make the launcher trace what it does to stderr.

    Inside the block, `resident` is the invocation's handle on the daemon, a `Resident`: which
    streams are terminals (`cliInput`, `cliOutput`, `cliError`), the name the executable was
    invoked as, the umask, the terminal's live size, a typed message `bus` between concurrent
    invocations, `cooked` and `concealed` blocks which ask the launcher for canonical terminal
    mode, `terminal` to run an editor or `sudo` on the client's terminal, and `retire()` to
    drain the daemon. `Installer.install` copies the executable onto the `PATH`, and `Upgrade`
    stages a signed replacement which the launcher verifies and swaps into place at the start
    of the next invocation, leaving the running daemon's clients undisturbed.

    The library is one source set, `ethereal.core`, for the JVM; it builds on `exoskeleton`
    (the CLI machinery: `Cli`, `Executive`, `arguments`, `execute`, `Help`), `coaxial` (the
    domain socket), `stratiform` (the BinTEL protocol), `surveillance` (watching the state
    files), `galilei`, `guillotine`, `profanity` and `turbulence`. A second source set,
    `ethereal.example`, holds the `run` and `fixture` entry points the tests and the tutorial
    use. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `environments` and
    `workingDirectories` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own.

refer ethereal.cli
refer ethereal.Resident
refer ethereal.Launcher
refer ethereal.Upgrade
refer ethereal.DaemonLogEvent

entry .cli   runs the body as a daemon: one resident JVM, serving each launcher invocation on its own connection
  detail
      `cli` is the daemonized counterpart of `application`: the same body, the same
      completions structure and the same `execute`, `arguments`, `Out` and `Exit`, but run by a
      process which stays resident and serves every invocation of the executable until it
      retires. It takes no arguments of its own — the launcher forwards the invocation's
      arguments, environment, working directory, standard streams, terminal size and signals —
      and the block runs once per client connection, with the invocation's `Resident over bus`,
      the executive's `Interface` (the `Cli`), its `Environment` and a `Monitor` as contextual
      values, so `resident`, `arguments`, `execute`, `Environment[Text](t"HOME")` and `task` all
      resolve inside it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      frame
        label A plain command-line application
        code
            import soundness.*

            import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
            import executives.completionsExecutive
            import interpreters.posixInterpreter
            import threads.virtualThreads

            @main
            def mytool(): Unit = application:
              execute:
                Out.println(t"Hello world")
                Exit.Ok
      frame
        label The same body, resident: `cli` in place of `application`
        code
            import soundness.*

            import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
            import executives.completionsExecutive
            import interpreters.posixInterpreter
            import threads.virtualThreads

            @main
            def mytool(): Unit = cli:
              execute:
                Out.println(t"Hello world")
                Exit.Ok
      frame
        label What the invocation knows about its client, through `resident`
        code
            import soundness.*

            import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
            import executives.completionsExecutive
            import interpreters.posixInterpreter
            import threads.virtualThreads

            @main
            def mytool(): Unit = cli:
              execute:
                if resident.cliOutput == Terminus.Terminal
                then Out.println(t"Hello, ${resident.invokedAs.or(t"world")}")
                else Out.println(t"Hello world")
                Exit.Ok
      frame
        label A typed bus between the daemon's concurrent invocations
        code
            import soundness.*

            import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
            import executives.completionsExecutive
            import interpreters.posixInterpreter
            import threads.virtualThreads

            enum Message:
              case ConfigChanged

            @main
            def mytool(): Unit = cli[Message]:
              execute:
                resident.broadcast(Message.ConfigChanged)
                Out.println(t"Hello world")
                Exit.Ok
      ##
      ```

      The type argument `bus` is the type of the messages the daemon's invocations exchange
      through `resident.bus` and `resident.broadcast`; it is `Matchable` and left unapplied
      for a daemon which exchanges none. The contextual values `cli` needs are the same as
      `application`'s — an `Executive` (`executives.completionsExecutive` for tab completions,
      the only executive which can produce `resident.help()`'s tree), an `Interpreter`, a
      `Threading` and a `Backstop` — plus a `Watcher`, with which it watches its state files
      (the default comes from `Watcher`'s companion). The block runs on the connection's task,
      so it may capture only *durable* capabilities — the daemon's monitor, logger and standard
      streams; an exclusive resource is opened and owned inside the block.

      The first invocation starts the daemon: the launcher starts the JVM with the properties
      `ethereal.name` (the executable's name), `ethereal.script` (its path),
      `ethereal.user.id`/`ethereal.user.name`, `ethereal.startTime`, `build.id` and
      `ethereal.environment=stdin`, and `cli` reads `DaemonEnvironment` from its standard
      input, writes its `acceptance` file, binds the socket, writes the `build` and `pid`
      files (the launcher waits for them before connecting) and starts a task which watches
      them and the launcher itself, so that a deleted state file or a rewritten executable
      ends the daemon promptly rather than leaving it serving broken no-ops. A JVM run
      without a launcher — `java -jar mytool.jar` — has no `ethereal.name`, prints _This
      application must be invoked through its XEK launcher_ with the `xek build` line to run,
      and exits with status 1.

      Each connection then opens with one `Launcher.Message`: an `Init` starts a `Session`
      and runs the block with a `Resident` built for it; `Shutdown` drains the daemon;
      `Verify` answers whether the launcher file still has the content the daemon was started
      from, and drains it if not; a document of another schema is logged as
      `DaemonLogEvent.ProtocolMismatch` and the connection closed, and a peer who is not the
      socket's owner, or an `Init` from another user, is refused (`PeerRefused`). An
      invocation which arrives while the daemon is draining is refused with exit status 2.

      Whatever the block does, the session ends with an exit status: an `Egress.Error` (a
      write to a stream whose reader has gone, `mytool | head -1` once `head` has exited)
      ends the invocation with status 141, as `SIGPIPE` would end a process; any other
      throwable — a `java.lang.Error` included — is logged as `DaemonLogEvent.Failure` and
      given to the `Backstop`, which reports it on the invocation's stderr and chooses the
      status. The daemon retires after six idle hours (`DaemonLogEvent.IdleTimeout`), and a
      drain — from `'{admin}' shutdown`, a stale verdict or `resident.retire()` — waits for the
      invocations in flight and at most thirty seconds, then exits. Arguments and environment
      entries which are not valid UTF-8 arrive with U+FFFD in place of the offending bytes,
      as the JVM itself would deliver them, and their original bytes are `resident.rawArgument`
      and `resident.rawEnvironment`.
  refer ethereal.resident
  refer ethereal.Resident
  refer ethereal.Launcher
  refer ethereal.DaemonLogEvent
  refer ethereal.Egress

entry .resident   the invocation's `Resident`, the handle on the daemon serving it, inside a `cli` block
    `resident` summons the `Resident over bus` which `cli` provides for the current
    invocation, so that `resident.cliInput`, `resident.invokedAs`, `resident.cooked`,
    `resident.broadcast` and the rest read naturally inside the block. It is a capability, so
    the result retains it: it cannot be stored past the invocation or passed to another.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
    import executives.completionsExecutive
    import interpreters.posixInterpreter
    import threads.virtualThreads

    @main
    def mytool(): Unit = cli:
      execute:
        Out.println(t"served by the daemon ${resident.pid.value.show}, started from ${resident.executable.encode}")
        Exit.Ok
    ```

entry .Resident   an invocation's handle on the daemon: what the launcher told it about the client, and the daemon's controls
  detail
      A `Resident` is the capability `cli` builds for each invocation and `resident` summons
      inside the block. It holds everything the daemon knows about the client — which it can
      only know from the launcher's `init` document, since the daemon holds a socket and not
      the client's process — together with the daemon's own controls: a typed message bus
      between concurrent invocations, the terminal-mode and run-on-terminal requests which
      travel back over the session, and `retire()`, which drains the daemon.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      @main
      def mytool(): Unit = cli:
        execute:
          resident.invokedAs.let(_.cut(t"/").last) match
            case t"gunzip" => Out.println(t"decompressing")
            case _         => Out.println(t"compressing")

          resident.windowSize.let:
            case (columns, rows) =>
              Out.println(t"a terminal of $columns×$rows")

          Exit.Ok
      ```

      It is an `Entrypoint` (`#script`, `#executable`, `#retire`), a `Umask.Provider` (its
      `#umask` is what the filesystem library applies to whatever the invocation creates) and
      a `Fdtable.Provider` (its `#fdtable` is how `/dev/stdin` or `/dev/fd/63` from
      `mytool <(…)` opens the *client's* descriptor, carried over the session, rather than the
      daemon's own). The type member `#Transport` is the bus's message type, written
      `Resident over Message`; the type `cli[Message]` provides. As an exclusive capability it
      cannot be stored past the invocation.

      Most of its constructor parameters are public `val`s the daemon fills in from the `init`
      document; the thunks among them (`#shutdown`, `#helpThunk`, `#setMode`, `#run`,
      `#sizeThunk`) are the raw hooks behind `#retire`, `#help`, `#cooked`, `#terminal` and
      `#windowSize`, which are the methods to call.
  refer ethereal.cli
  refer ethereal.resident
  refer ethereal.Terminus
  refer ethereal.Tty
  entry #Transport   the type of the messages this daemon's invocations exchange over the bus
      Abstract, bounded by `Matchable`, and fixed by the type argument to `cli`: inside
      `cli[Message]` the resident is a `Resident over Message`, so `#broadcast` takes a
      `Message` and `#bus` yields them. A daemon with no type argument exchanges nothing.
  entry #bus   the messages broadcast by the daemon's other invocations, as they arrive
      A `Chain[Transport]` which grows as other invocations call `#broadcast`: reading it
      blocks until the next message. An invocation's own broadcasts are not delivered to it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      enum Message:
        case ConfigChanged

      @main
      def watch(): Unit = cli[Message]:
        execute:
          resident.bus.each:
            case Message.ConfigChanged => Out.println(t"another invocation changed the configuration")

          Exit.Ok
      ```
  entry #broadcast   sends a message to every other invocation of the daemon
      Delivers the message to the `#bus` of every invocation currently connected except the
      sender's. This is how a running `watch` learns that another invocation just changed the
      configuration.
  entry #retire   asks the daemon to go once this invocation has finished
      The daemon stops accepting invocations, lets those in flight finish (for at most thirty
      seconds), and exits; the next invocation starts a fresh daemon. The `'{admin}' shutdown`
      subcommand does the same from the shell. It overrides `Entrypoint#retire`, which does
      nothing for a plain application, so a tool written against `Entrypoint` works under
      both.
  entry #shutdown   the thunk `#retire` calls: the daemon's drain
  entry #pid   the client process's pid, as the launcher reported it
      The pid of the *launcher* process serving the user's shell, not the daemon's; it keys
      the daemon's table of connected clients, and `DaemonLogEvent.Init` and `CloseConnection`
      carry it.
  entry #cliInput   whether the client's stdin is a terminal or a pipe
      A `Terminus`: `Terminal` when the launcher reported stdin attached to a terminal, `Pipe`
      otherwise. The daemon cannot tell for itself — its streams are the launcher's socket —
      so this is the launcher's report and nothing else. `#cooked` and `#concealed` are no-ops
      unless this is `Terminal`.
  entry #cliOutput   whether the client's stdout is a terminal or a pipe
      Reported separately from stdin, since they genuinely differ: `mytool > file` run from a
      terminal has stdin on the terminal and stdout on a file. When stdout is not a terminal
      the invocation's `Termcap` has no width, so nothing wraps.
  entry #cliError   whether the client's stderr is a terminal or a pipe
  entry #executable   the path of the launcher executable the invocation ran
      The `script` field of the `init` document, as a `Path on Local`: the canonical path of
      the executable. `Installer.install` copies this file onto the `PATH`.
  entry #script   the executable's name, as the daemon was given it
      The `ethereal.name` property the launcher starts the daemon with: the command's name,
      which also names the daemon's state directory and its data directory.
  entry #startTime   when the daemon started, in epoch milliseconds
      From the `ethereal.startTime` property, or the JVM's clock when there is no launcher.
      `#started` and `#uptime` read it as typed values.
  entry #helpThunk   the thunk behind `#help`: the executive's help tree, or `Unset`
  entry #setMode   the hook behind `#cooked` and `#concealed`: asks the launcher for a `Tty` mode
      Sends a `Launcher.Message.Mode` over the session. Only the launcher can change the
      client's terminal, and a launcher whose stdin is not a terminal it owns ignores the
      request. Prefer `#cooked` and `#concealed`, which restore raw mode afterwards.
  entry #run   the hook behind `#terminal`: a command, its arguments and a directory, to the status it ended with
  entry #invokedAs   the name the executable was invoked by: `argv[0]` as the caller supplied it
      `Unset` under a launcher which did not report it. One executable installed under
      several names by symbolic links can dispatch on this, while every alias shares one warm
      daemon; the path is as the caller wrote it, so take its last segment for the bare name.
  entry #sizeThunk   the thunk behind `#windowSize`: the terminal's size as last measured
  entry #umask   the invocation's file-creation mask, if the launcher reported it
      A galilei `Umask`, parsed from the octal `umask` field of the `init` document. Through
      `Umask.Provider` the filesystem library applies it to whatever the invocation creates,
      so a file made by the daemon has the mode the client's shell would have given it, not
      the daemon's own `077`.
  entry #fdtable   the client's descriptor table, for paths which name a descriptor
      An `Fdtable` the filesystem library consults before it opens anything: `/dev/stdin`,
      `/dev/stdout`, `/dev/stderr`, `/dev/fd/N` and `/proc/self/fd/N` name the *client's*
      descriptors — the session's own streams for 0, 1 and 2; a regular file behind a
      descriptor by its real path; a pipe such as `mytool <(…)` carried as a stream once the
      launcher is asked for it — and a descriptor the client did not advertise names nothing,
      whatever the daemon's own table holds at that number. `Unset` without a launcher.
  entry #raws   the native bytes of each argument, environment entry or working directory whose text lost something
      The `Launcher.Raw` records of the `init` document: empty in the ordinary case, where
      every value was valid UTF-8. `#rawArgument`, `#rawEnvironment` and
      `#rawWorkingDirectory` look them up.
  entry #windowSize   the client terminal's size as (columns, rows), as the launcher last measured it
      Measured at connection and again with every `WINCH` and `CONT`, so it is live; the
      invocation's `Termcap` reads the same measurement, so tables and wrapped text fit the
      terminal as it is now. `Unset` when the client's stdout is not a terminal, or no size
      was reported.
  entry #cooked   runs a block with the client's terminal in canonical mode, with echo
      The launcher puts the terminal into raw mode before it connects, so that keypresses can
      be forwarded to an interactive session; that is wrong for a command which just wants a
      line of input, since there is no echo and Backspace arrives as a byte inside the line.
      `cooked` asks the launcher for canonical mode for the duration of the block — the
      terminal driver then provides echo and line editing — and restores raw mode afterwards,
      in a `finally`. A no-op when `#cliInput` is not a terminal, and silently ineffective
      under a launcher too old to offer the control channel.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      @main
      def ask(): Unit = cli:
        execute:
          val name = resident.cooked:
            Out.println(t"Name?")
            java.io.BufferedReader(java.io.InputStreamReader(summon[Stdio].in)).readLine().nn.tt

          Out.println(t"Hello, $name")
          Exit.Ok
      ```
  entry #concealed   runs a block in canonical mode with nothing echoed: how a password is read
      As `#cooked`, with the terminal driver's line editing but no echo, for the duration of
      the block; raw mode is restored afterwards. A no-op when stdin is not a terminal.
  entry #terminal   runs a command on the client's terminal, and gives the status it ended with
      For an editor, a pager, `ssh` or `sudo`: programs which need the terminal, which the
      daemon's process, detached from any terminal, cannot reach. The launcher stops
      forwarding the terminal's input and holds the invocation's output, restores the
      terminal's saved state, runs the command with the client's environment and standard
      streams — in `pwd` if given — and, when it ends, puts the terminal back and reports
      the status: the exit code, 128 plus the signal it died of, or 127 if it could not be
      run, which is also the answer when the client's stdin is not a terminal. One command
      runs at a time. Its output goes to the terminal, not the invocation; to capture a
      program's output, run it in this process with `sh"…".exec`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      @main
      def edit(): Unit = cli:
        execute:
          val status: Int = resident.terminal(t"vi", List(t"-R", t"notes.txt"))
          if status == 0 then Exit.Ok else Exit.Fail(status)
      ```
  entry #rawArgument   the operating system's own bytes of the argument at an index, where its text lost something
      An argument which is not valid UTF-8 reaches `arguments` with U+FFFD in place of the
      offending bytes, as the JVM would deliver it; this gives the bytes as given — on Unix
      the bytes themselves, on Windows the UTF-16 code units, little-endian — for the
      argument at `index`, counted from 0. `Unset` for an argument its text carries exactly,
      which is the ordinary case, so a caller falls back to the text.
  entry #rawEnvironment   the operating system's own bytes of the environment entry (`NAME=value`) at an index
      As `#rawArgument`, for the entry at `index` of the environment the launcher forwarded;
      `Unset` for an entry whose text is exact.
  entry #rawWorkingDirectory   the working directory's bytes, where its text could not carry them
  entry #help   the structured help tree for this command, from the completions executive
      Generated lazily and memoized by the daemon: the application's pure portion is re-run
      in tab-completion mode to discover its subcommand and flag tree. Only
      `executives.completionsExecutive` can produce the tree; under another executive this
      is a root `Help` with the command's name and nothing beneath it.
  entry #localHelp   the help tree narrowed to the subcommands this invocation has matched so far
      Needs the invocation's `Cli`: `help().local(cli.matches)`, so that a section of the
      application documents only itself, falling back to the full tree when nothing has
      been matched or the matched path is absent from it.
  entry #buildId   the build id of the executable the daemon was started from; zero without a launcher
      The 64-bit `build.id` property the launcher passes (xek's `--build-id`), or a
      `build.id` classpath resource when there is no launcher, or zero. Compare it with the
      build id of a release on offer before staging an `Upgrade`.
  entry #started   when the daemon started, as any instant type made from epoch milliseconds
      `resident.started[Instant]`, for any `Instantiable across Instants from Long`.
  entry #uptime   how long the daemon has been running, as any duration type made from nanoseconds
      `resident.uptime[Duration]`, for any `Instantiable across Durations from Long`; never
      negative.

entry .Launcher   the launcher-to-daemon protocol: the `ethereal-launcher` TEL schema, its messages and their BinTEL codec
  detail
      `Launcher` is the contract between an Ethereal launcher — the Rust runner published by
      `propensive/xek` — and its daemon. An invocation is one connection: it opens with an
      `init` document and then carries, in both directions, the documents of a session —
      `data` chunks of every stream, `end`, `credit`, `open`, `signal`/`signal-ack`, `mode`,
      `run`/`exited` and `closed` — until the daemon's `exit-status` ends it; `verify` and
      `shutdown` are asked on short connections of their own. The TEL schema is the
      specification: its text is the resource `ethereal/ethereal-launcher.tel` in ethereal's
      own jar, byte for byte xek's `spec/ethereal-launcher.tel`, and `.Message` mirrors it
      variant for variant. Each document on the wire is one framed BinTEL document — magic,
      length, the schema's 33-byte `.signature`, body — and both sides compare the signature
      before reading a field, so a launcher and a daemon built against different schemas
      refuse each other at the first document (`DaemonLogEvent.ProtocolMismatch`) rather than
      misread fields. The daemon reads from an untrusted peer, so a declared length over
      `.maximumLength` is refused before it is acted on.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val frame: Data = Launcher.encode(Launcher.Message.ExitStatus(42))
      Launcher.decode(frame)   // Launcher.Message.ExitStatus(42)
      Launcher.signature.length   // 33
      ```

      An application never speaks the protocol itself: `cli` and `Session` do. The object is
      public so that a tool which drives a daemon directly, or a test which pins the wire
      bytes against the runner's, can build and read the same documents.
  refer ethereal.cli
  refer ethereal.Session
  entry .schemaText   the text of the `ethereal-launcher` TEL schema, read from ethereal's own jar
      The resource `ethereal/ethereal-launcher.tel`, read once through ethereal's own
      classloader (`Classloader[Launcher.type]`), so the first thread to need it may carry
      any context classloader. Its absence is a packaging error and throws.
  entry .schema   the parsed and validated `Tels` schema the codec encodes against
      Parsed once from `.schemaText`; a malformed schema is a programming error, not a runtime
      condition.
  entry .signature   the schema's 33-byte palimpsest signature, carried by every document
      Compared byte for byte on receipt: `.decode` gives `Unset` for a document carrying
      another signature. The daemon also writes it into its `acceptance` state file, for a
      launcher to consult before it connects.
  entry .maximumLength   the longest document the daemon will read: one mebibyte
      A declared length beyond it makes `.readDocument` give `Unset` without reading the body.
      A chunk is at most `.maximumChunk`, and an invocation's environment and arguments fit
      comfortably.
  entry .maximumChunk   the most bytes one `data` document carries: 65536
      Also the credit each stream opens with in each direction (`Session.window`).
  entry .encode   a message as one framed BinTEL document
      Magic, length varint, `.signature`, body. A `Data` message is framed by hand, its bytes
      going straight into the frame with no BASE-256 text in between, since every chunk of
      every stream passes this way. Cannot fail: the elements are built against the
      library's own schema.
  entry .decode   the message a framed document carries, or `Unset`
      `Unset` when the document is malformed, carries another schema's signature, or does
      not fit `.Message`; a `Data` document is read by hand, as `.encode` writes it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Launcher.decode(Launcher.encode(Launcher.Message.Mode(true, true)))   // Launcher.Message.Mode(true, true)
      ```
  entry .readDocument   reads exactly one framed document from a `java.io.InputStream`
      Reads the four-byte magic, the length varint and then the declared number of bytes,
      leaving whatever follows unread, and gives the whole frame for `.decode`. `Unset` at
      end of input, on a malformed header, on a length over `.maximumLength`, or when the
      stream ends before the declared length.
  entry .Descriptor   a file descriptor the client holds, as the `init` document advertises it
    detail
        The client's descriptors are numbered as the client sees them (0, 1 and 2 among
        them), each with its direction, its kind and, for a regular file, the real path the
        daemon may open directly; anything else is reached by opening the descriptor as a
        stream named by its number, with a `Launcher.Message.Open`. `Resident#fdtable`
        consults these.
    entry #fd   the descriptor's number, as the client holds it
    entry #direction   `r`, `w` or `rw`, as the descriptor was opened
    entry #kind   `file`, `pipe`, `tty`, `socket` or `other`
    entry #path   the real path, for a regular file; `Unset` otherwise
  entry .Raw   a value of the `init` document as the operating system gave it, where its text lost something
    detail
        An argument or environment entry which is not valid UTF-8 (or on Windows holds an
        unpaired surrogate), or the working directory, is sent as text with U+FFFD in place
        of what could not be carried, and again as a `Raw`: the platform's own bytes — bytes
        on Unix, UTF-16 code units, little-endian, on Windows. `Resident#rawArgument`,
        `Resident#rawEnvironment` and `Resident#rawWorkingDirectory` look them up.
    entry #kind   `argument`, `environment` or `pwd`
    entry #index   the position among the arguments or environment entries, from 0; `Unset` for `pwd`
    entry #bytes   the platform's own bytes of the value
  entry .Message   the documents of the protocol, one case per variant of the schema
    detail
        The fifteen variants of the schema's root `select`, in its order, so that each case's
        index is its variant index on the wire. The launcher sends `Init`, `Data`, `End`,
        `Credit`, `Signal`, `Closed`, `Exited`, `Verify` and `Shutdown`; the daemon sends
        `Data`, `End`, `Credit`, `Open`, `SignalAck`, `Mode`, `Closed`, `Run`, `ExitStatus`
        and `Verdict`. `Verify` and `Shutdown` carry no fields.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val messages: List[Launcher.Message] =
          List
            ( Launcher.Message.Data(t"stdout", t"hello".in[Data]),
              Launcher.Message.Credit(t"stdin", 65536L),
              Launcher.Message.Signal(t"WINCH", 80, 24),
              Launcher.Message.Mode(canonical = true, echo = false),
              Launcher.Message.ExitStatus(0) )

        messages.map(Launcher.encode).map(Launcher.decode)
        ```

        `Verify` asks, on a connection of its own, whether the launcher file the daemon
        started from still has the content it remembers (sent when the file's mtime disagrees
        with the daemon's `build` file); the daemon answers with a `Verdict` and, if stale,
        drains itself so that the launcher's fresh daemon can take its place. `Shutdown` asks
        the daemon to accept no further invocations, let those in flight finish, and end; it
        is not answered.
    entry .Init   a new invocation, opening the session which carries it
      detail
          The first document of every invocation's connection. The three tty flags say which
          of the client's streams are attached to a terminal, which the daemon cannot
          determine for itself; the user is the platform's identifier (numeric on Unix, a SID
          on Windows), and an `Init` from a user other than the daemon's own is refused with
          exit status 2. A daemon which is draining refuses it the same way.
      entry #pid   the client process's pid
      entry #uid   the platform's identifier for the user: numeric on Unix, a SID on Windows
      entry #username   the user's login name
      entry #script   the canonical path of the executable
      entry #pwd   the invocation's working directory
      entry #stdinTty   whether the client's stdin is a terminal
      entry #stdoutTty   whether the client's stdout is a terminal
      entry #stderrTty   whether the client's stderr is a terminal
      entry #arguments   the invocation's arguments, as text
      entry #environment   the invocation's environment, each entry as `NAME=value`
      entry #invokedAs   `argv[0]` as the caller supplied it, for a multi-call binary to dispatch on
      entry #umask   the invocation's umask, in octal, if reported
      entry #columns   the terminal's width when stdout is a terminal
      entry #rows   the terminal's height when stdout is a terminal
      entry #inputCodepage   the Windows console's input code page, if any
      entry #outputCodepage   the Windows console's output code page, if any
      entry #descriptors   the descriptors the client holds, which the daemon may open as streams
      entry #raws   the values whose text form lost something, as the operating system gave them
    entry .Data   one chunk of a stream, of at most `Launcher.maximumChunk` bytes
      detail
          Sent in both directions. The stream is `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr` or the number of
          an open descriptor, and a sender never has more bytes outstanding on a stream than
          the credit it holds for it.
      entry #stream   `stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr` or a descriptor's number
      entry #bytes   the chunk's bytes
    entry .End   the named stream has ended, after every chunk sent before it
      detail
          End-of-file for the daemon's reader of stdin or of a descriptor opened to read, or
          the daemon's close of a descriptor opened to write. Nothing more is sent on the
          stream.
      entry #stream   the stream which has ended
    entry .Credit   the receiver of a stream can take this many more bytes of it
      detail
          Flow control: each stream opens with 65536 bytes of credit in each direction, and a
          receiver grants more as it consumes, in halves of the window rather than per chunk.
          A producer whose client is slow to drain stalls only its own stream, so
          `mytool | less`, paused, still receives a signal.
      entry #stream   the stream the credit is for
      entry #bytes   how many more bytes may be sent, in addition to any credit granted before
    entry .Open   asks the launcher to start carrying a descriptor the client advertised
      detail
          Sent by the daemon when the invocation opens `/dev/fd/N`: as `Data` documents from
          the client if the descriptor was opened to read, or by writing `Data` documents the
          daemon sends if opened to write. Not answered; the stream simply begins. A
          descriptor the daemon did not advertise, or opens again while open, is ignored.
      entry #stream   the descriptor's number
    entry .Signal   a signal the client received, or a Windows console control event
      detail
          Named without its `SIG` prefix. `WINCH` and `CONT` carry the terminal's current
          size; a Windows close, logoff or shutdown carries the milliseconds the system allows
          before it ends the client regardless. The daemon dispatches it to the invocation's
          traps as a profanity `Signal` and answers with `SignalAck`; the launcher sends no
          further signal until it has the answer.
      entry #name   the signal's name without its `SIG` prefix, or the Windows event
      entry #columns   the terminal's width, with `WINCH` and `CONT`
      entry #rows   the terminal's height, with `WINCH` and `CONT`
      entry #deadline   the milliseconds a Windows close, logoff or shutdown allows
    entry .SignalAck   whether the invocation accepted the signal last sent
      detail
          `accept` is true when a trap answered `SignalResponse.Accept`; false — a trap which
          rejected or deferred it, no trap, or a trap too slow to answer — lets the launcher
          fall back to the operating system's default for the signal.
      entry #accept   whether the invocation accepted the signal
    entry .Mode   asks the launcher to put the client's terminal into canonical or raw mode
      detail
          `canonical` asks for cooked mode, with echo if `echo` is also set; canonical
          without echo is how a password is read; neither flag is raw mode. The launcher
          answers nothing, and leaves a terminal it does not own as it is.
          `Resident#cooked` and `Resident#concealed` send it.
      entry #canonical   cooked mode, or raw when false
      entry #echo   whether the terminal echoes what is typed
    entry .Closed   the named stream has lost its reader
      detail
          From the launcher: the client's stdout or stderr, or a descriptor opened to write,
          could not be written; the invocation's further writes to that stream raise
          `Egress.Error`, as a broken pipe would. From the daemon: the invocation closed a
          descriptor it opened to read before reading it to its end, and the launcher stops
          carrying it.
      entry #stream   the stream whose reader is gone
    entry .ExitStatus   the invocation's exit status, ending the session
      detail
          The last document the daemon writes, after every chunk of every stream; the
          connection closes once the launcher has it.
      entry #code   the exit status
    entry .Verdict   the daemon's answer to `Verify`: whether its launcher file is unchanged
      detail
          `fresh` when the launcher file's content is what the daemon was started from (a
          metadata-only change such as `touch`, which the daemon remembers so the hash is not
          repeated); otherwise the daemon drains itself and the launcher, awaiting its death,
          starts a fresh one.
      entry #fresh   whether the launcher file still has the content the daemon remembers
    entry .Run   asks the launcher to run a command on the client's terminal
      detail
          For an editor, a pager, `ssh` or `sudo`. The launcher stops carrying the terminal's
          input, holds the invocation's output, restores the terminal's saved state, runs the
          command with the client's environment and streams — in `pwd`, if given — and
          answers with `Exited` when it ends. A client whose stdin is not a terminal it owns
          answers `Exited(127)` at once. `Resident#terminal` sends it.
      entry #command   the command to run
      entry #arguments   its arguments
      entry #pwd   the directory to run it in, if not the invocation's
    entry .Exited   the status the command the daemon asked to run ended with
      detail
          The exit code, 128 plus the signal it died of, or 127 if the command could not be
          run.
      entry #code   the status

entry .Session   the daemon's half of an invocation's session: one connection, a reader thread and a writer thread
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `cli` makes one per `Init` and the invocation's `Stdio` is built on
      its streams. It carries every stream of the invocation as `Launcher.Message.Data`
      documents under per-stream credit, with the control traffic in between: the reader
      never waits on anything but the socket — it deposits each chunk with its stream, acts
      on each control document at once, and hands a signal to a thread of its own, since a
      trap may sleep — and the writer is the only thread which writes the connection, control
      documents ahead of data. A stream's producer waits for credit outside any lock, so a
      stream the client is slow to drain stalls only its own producer. Its companion holds
      the protocol's figures, `.window` and `.chunk`, and `.governed`, which says which of a
      process's own descriptor paths name a descriptor.
  refer ethereal.Launcher
  entry .window   the credit each stream opens with in each direction: 65536 bytes
  entry .chunk   the most bytes one `data` document carries: `Launcher.maximumChunk`
  entry .governed   the descriptor number a path such as `/dev/stdin` or `/dev/fd/63` names, if it names one
      `/dev/stdin`, `/dev/stdout` and `/dev/stderr` are 0, 1 and 2; `/dev/fd/N` and
      `/proc/self/fd/N` are `N`, for a decimal of fewer than eight digits; any other path
      is `Unset`. Any such path is the client's table to answer for, whether or not the
      client holds the descriptor, so the daemon's own table is never reached through it.
  entry #send   queues a control document for the writer, ahead of any data
  entry #finish   ends the session from this side: `exit-status` behind everything queued before it
  entry #awaitWritten   waits for the writer's queue to drain after `#finish`, so the status reaches the launcher
  entry #stdin   the invocation's stdin, a `SessionInput` fed by the launcher's `data` documents
  entry #stdout   the invocation's stdout, a `SessionOutput` sent under credit
  entry #stderr   the invocation's stderr, a `SessionOutput` sent under credit
  entry #windowSize   the client terminal's size as the launcher last measured it, as an `Atomic.Ref`
  entry #terminal   runs a command on the client's terminal with a `Run` document, and waits for `Exited`
      One at a time; 127 if the launcher is gone or never answers.
  entry #start   starts the writer and reader threads
  entry #fdtable   the client's descriptors as an `Fdtable`, consulted for `/dev/stdin`-style paths

entry .SessionInput   the chunks of one stream the launcher sends, as a `java.io.InputStream`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the stream behind an invocation's stdin, or a descriptor opened to
      read. Chunks are deposited by the session's reader with `#push` and taken by whatever
      reads the invocation's stdin; the stream grants the launcher more credit in halves of
      the window as it consumes, so no more of the client's input is taken than the
      invocation asked for.
  entry #push   deposits a chunk; ignored once the stream has ended
  entry #end   marks the stream ended: a read past the deposited chunks gives end-of-file
  entry #isEnded   whether the launcher has sent `end` for the stream
  entry #read   blocks until a chunk is available or the stream has ended
  entry #available   the bytes deposited and not yet read

entry .SessionOutput   a stream the daemon sends, buffered up to a chunk and sent under credit
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the stream behind an invocation's stdout or stderr, or a descriptor
      opened to write. Bytes are buffered up to `Session.chunk` and sent on `flush` — which
      the invocation's `PrintStream`s do after every write — or when full, each chunk once
      the launcher has granted credit for it. Once the launcher reports the stream closed, or
      is gone, a write raises `Egress.Error`.
  entry #severed   set once the stream's reader at the client is gone: every later write raises `Egress.Error`
  entry #write   buffers bytes, flushing each full chunk
  entry #flush   sends the buffered bytes as chunks, waiting for credit on each
  entry #close   flushes; the connection is closed by the session, not the stream

entry .Client   what the daemon keeps of an invocation outside its session: its bus and its `Cli`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the daemon's record of a connected client, keyed by the client's
      pid, which holds the client's place on the message bus and the promise of its `Cli`,
      which the session's signal dispatch may need before the invocation has made it.
      `Client[bus](pid)` makes one whose `Topic` is the bus's message type.
  entry .apply   a client record for a pid, typed to the daemon's bus
  entry #pid   the client process's pid
  entry #Topic   the bus's message type
  entry #invocation   the promise of the invocation's `Cli`, completed once the invocation has made it
  entry #bus   the relay the client's broadcasts from other invocations arrive on
  entry #receive   delivers a message broadcast by another invocation

entry .DaemonEnvironment   the environment the daemon was launched with, as the launcher sent it
  detail
      The launcher starts a daemon with a sanitized environment of its own — only `PATH`,
      `HOME`, `USER`, `LOGNAME`, `SHELL`, `LANG`, `LC_*`, `TZ`, `TMPDIR`, the `XDG_*` base
      directories, the JVM's option variables and `ETHEREAL_DEBUG` — so that the daemon's
      state does not depend on who happened to start it, and sends the environment it was
      itself started with on the daemon's standard input, each entry `NAME=value` ended by a
      NUL byte, as the property `ethereal.environment=stdin` announces. `DaemonEnvironment`
      is that environment, held in memory and never written anywhere; without the property
      (an older launcher, or a JVM run directly) it is the JVM's own environment, which is
      then the launching one.

      Few things want this rather than the *invocation's* environment, which arrives with
      each invocation and is the `Environment` given inside a `cli` block (and the choice
      `environments.daemonClientEnvironment`); it is chosen explicitly, as the given
      `environments.daemonEnvironment`, imported by name.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.daemonEnvironment

      safely(Environment.home[Text])
      ```
  refer ethereal.cli
  refer ethereal.LazyEnvironment
  entry .capture   reads the environment the launcher sent, if it has not been read already
      `cli` calls it as the daemon starts, before the socket is bound: the launcher sends the
      environment only while it waits for the socket to appear, and the daemon's standard
      input is read to its end.
  entry .variable   the value of one variable, or `Unset`
  entry .entries   every variable, as a `Map`

entry .LazyEnvironment   an `Environment` over a list of `NAME=value` entries, parsed on first use
  detail
      The `Environment` of an invocation inside a `cli` block: the entries the launcher
      forwarded in the `init` document, split on the first `=` when the environment is first
      consulted (an entry with no `=` is dropped). It is what the given
      `environments.daemonClientEnvironment` resolves to, and what `Environment[Text](name)`
      reads inside the block.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val environment: Environment = LazyEnvironment(List(t"HOME=/home/jon", t"SHELL=/bin/zsh"))
      environment.variable(t"HOME")   // t"/home/jon"
      ```
  entry #variable   the value of one variable, or `Unset`
  entry #entries   every variable, as a `Map`

entry .DaemonLogEvent   what the daemon logs to syslog as it serves, drains and retires
  detail
      Every event the daemon logs under the `ethereal` syslog identity, each tagged with the
      `Log` category it belongs to (`Log.Process`, `Log.Network`, `Log.Protocol`,
      `Log.Filesystem`, `Log.Scheduler` or `Log.Runtime`). The companion gives the
      `Communicable` which renders each as a message and the `Message transcribes
      DaemonLogEvent` which lets a logger take them, both in implicit scope.

      The singleton cases: `Shutdown` (shutting down); `Termination` (terminating the client
      connection: the launcher was rewritten or a stale verdict given, logged as a warning
      before the daemon exits); `IdleTimeout` (shutting down after six idle hours);
      `NewCli` (instantiating a new CLI, at `fine`); `UnrecognizedMessage` (a document that
      is not a launcher message, or one which belongs inside a session arriving outside
      one); `ProtocolMismatch` (a document of another launcher protocol schema: a launcher
      built against a different contract); and `Draining` (accepting no further invocations;
      exiting when those in flight end). `Installer.install` logs `WriteExecutable`, and
      `cli` logs the rest.
  refer ethereal.cli
  entry .transcribable   lets a `Logger` take daemon events as `Message`s
  entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
  entry .WriteExecutable   the installer is writing the executable: _writing the executable to `location`_
    entry #location   the path being written
  entry .Failure   an invocation threw: _the invocation failed: `error`_
    detail
        Logged as a failure before the throwable is given to the `Backstop`, which reports
        it on the invocation's stderr and chooses the exit status.
    entry #error   the throwable, rendered as text
  entry .ReceivedSignal   the launcher forwarded a signal: _received signal `signal`_
    entry #signal   the `UnixSignal` or `WindowsSignal` received
  entry .CloseConnection   an invocation ended: _closing the connection from `pid`_
    entry #pid   the client's pid
  entry .Init   an invocation began: _initialising `pid`_
    entry #pid   the client's pid
  entry .PeerRefused   a connection from another user was refused: _refusing a connection from another user: `user`_
    detail
        The socket admits only its owner: a peer whose kernel-reported user is not the
        daemon's, or an `Init` claiming another user id, is refused without being read.
    entry #user   the peer's user
  entry .Refused   an invocation arrived while the daemon was draining: _refusing invocation `pid`: the daemon is shutting down_
    entry #pid   the client's pid
  entry .Closed   a stream's reader at the client has gone: _the invocation's `stream` has no reader_
    entry #stream   `stdout`, `stderr` or a descriptor's number

entry .Egress   an invocation's stdout or stderr: a stream which fails once the launcher reports its reader gone
  detail
      The `java.io.OutputStream` behind an invocation's `Out` and `Err`: it delegates to the
      session's stream until the launcher sends `closed` for it — `mytool | head -1`, once
      `head` has exited — after which every write raises `.Error`, so that a program which
      writes until it cannot ends, as it would were it writing a pipe. `cli` catches the
      error and ends the invocation with status 141, as `SIGPIPE` would end a process (128
      plus 13), without consulting the `Backstop`, which would write to the same stream.
  refer ethereal.cli
  entry .Error   raised by a write to an invocation's stdout or stderr whose reader has gone
    detail
        _the invocation's `stream` has no reader_: the daemon's equivalent of the `EPIPE` a
        process writing a pipe would get. It is deliberately not an `IOException`, which
        `java.io.PrintStream` would swallow: it must reach the invocation, which `cli` ends
        with exit status 141. An invocation which wants to survive the loss of its output
        catches it as `case error: Egress.Error`.
    entry #stream   `stdout` or `stderr`
  entry #write   writes to the underlying stream, or raises `Egress.Error` once the reader is gone

entry .Installer   copies the running executable onto the `PATH`, from inside the daemon
  detail
      An application built with `xek build` is one file — the native launcher stub, a
      configuration record and the JAR — and `Installer.install` puts a copy of it where the
      shell will find it: the first writable directory on the `PATH`, preferring the XDG
      `bin` directory, `/usr/local/bin` and `/usr/bin`, or a `target` of the caller's
      choosing. It is what the built-in `'{admin}' install` subcommand runs, and an
      application may offer it under a name of its own.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.daemonClientEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      @main
      def mytool(): Unit = cli:
        arguments match
          case Argument("install") :: Nil =>
            execute:
              recover:
                case Install.Error(reason) =>
                  Err.println(m"could not install: $reason")
                  Exit.Fail(1)
              . protect:
                  Out.println(Installer.install().communicate)
                  Exit.Ok

          case _ =>
            execute(Exit.Ok)
      ```

      The copy omits the launcher's embedded payload (the runner stubs for other platforms,
      whose sizes the launcher passes as `ethereal.payloadSize` and `ethereal.jarSize`) and
      rebases the JAR's ZIP64 locator to its new offset, so the installed file is a plain
      launcher for the platform it runs on. It is written to a hidden sibling and renamed
      over the target, which gives the target a fresh inode: on macOS the code-signing
      cache is per-vnode, and overwriting a previously-executed binary's content in place
      poisons it so that every later exec is killed with `SIGKILL` — the same trap awaits a
      `cp new old` over an installed launcher by hand; use `mv`, or remove it first.
  refer ethereal.Resident
  entry .Result   what `Installer.install` did
    detail
        `Installed(script, path)` — _The `script` command was installed to `path`._;
        `AlreadyOnPath(script, path)` — _The `script` command is already installed at
        `path`._, when `command -v` already resolves the name to the running executable and
        `force` was not given; and `PathNotWritable` — _No directory on the PATH environment
        variable was writable_, when the temporary file could not be created. The
        companion's `Communicable` renders each as a message.
    entry .communicable   renders a result as a message
    entry .AlreadyOnPath   the command already resolves to the running executable
      entry #script   the command's name
      entry #path   the path it is installed at
    entry .Installed   the executable was copied to a directory on the `PATH`
      entry #script   the command's name
      entry #path   the path it was installed to
  entry .candidateTargets   the writable directories on the `PATH`, best first
      Every existing, writable directory of the `PATH` variable (read from the `Environment`
      in scope — the invocation's, inside a `cli` block), ordered with the XDG `bin`
      directory first, then `/usr/local/bin`, `/usr/bin`, `/usr/local/sbin`, `/opt/bin` and
      `/bin`, and any other in `PATH` order after them. Needs a `Resident`, a `System`, a
      `Tactic[Install.Error]` (raised with reason `Environment` for an unreadable `PATH` or
      an invalid path in it, or `Io` for a filesystem failure) and a `DaemonLogEvent is
      Loggable`.
  entry .install   installs the running executable onto the `PATH`, or to a chosen directory
      `install(force, target)`: `target` is the directory to install into, by default the
      first of `.candidateTargets`; `force` installs even when the command already resolves
      to the running executable. Needs a `Resident` (for `executable` and `script`), the
      `Environment` to read `PATH` from, an `Effectful` (it is a side effect on the
      filesystem), `Diagnostics`, a `Tactic[Install.Error]` and a `DaemonLogEvent is
      Loggable`, and logs `DaemonLogEvent.WriteExecutable` as it writes. `Install.Error` is
      raised with reason `Environment` when the name, path, `PATH` or the launcher's size
      properties cannot be read, and `Io` when the copy, the ZIP rebase or the rename
      fails.

entry .Upgrade   stages a signed replacement executable for the launcher to verify and apply
  detail
      Self-upgrade, from the daemon's side. An application stages the bytes of a complete
      signed executable — a release built with `xek build --build-id … --public-key …
      --app-id …` and signed with `xek sign` — as `.pending` in its data directory
      (`$XDG_DATA_HOME/<name>/`, or `~/.local/share/<name>/`; `%LOCALAPPDATA%\<name>\` on
      Windows), and the launcher does the rest at the start of the command's **next**
      invocation: it verifies the candidate against the keys and application identifier in
      the *running* executable's record, requires its build id to be newer, swaps it into
      place, keeps the replaced executable beside it, re-execs, and records what it did in
      `.upgrade-result`. Its staleness check then displaces the running daemon by itself, so
      nothing here exits or respawns anything and the clients the daemon is serving carry
      on undisturbed. The Scala side verifies nothing: verification happens in the launcher,
      before the JVM starts, so no application code sits on the trust boundary.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.daemonClientEnvironment
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def upgrade(): Text raises Upgrade.Error =
        if !Upgrade.enabled then t"this build cannot be upgraded"
        else if Upgrade.pending then t"the upgrade will be applied the next time the command is run"
        else
          Upgrade.stage(url"https://releases.example.com/myapp.signed".fetch())
          t"the upgrade will be applied the next time the command is run"
      ```

      Every method needs an `Environment` and a `System` (the invocation's environment,
      `environments.daemonClientEnvironment`, inside a `cli` block) to find the data
      directory and the `ethereal.name` property. `.outcome` reads what the launcher did with
      the last candidate, and `.acknowledge` clears it so that each outcome is reported once;
      `.enabled` says whether the running executable's launcher would accept a signed
      candidate at all, and `Resident#buildId` is the running build's identifier, to compare
      with a release on offer.
  refer ethereal.Resident
  refer ethereal.cli
  entry .stage   writes a candidate executable as `.pending`, for the launcher to apply next time
      Takes any source which is `Readable to Data` — a file, an HTTP response, a stream —
      reads it whole, writes it to a temporary file beside `.pending` and renames it into
      place, so a launcher starting concurrently never reads a partial candidate. It returns
      normally: the upgrade takes effect the next time the command is run, which the
      application should tell the user. Raises `Upgrade.Error` with reason
      `CannotResolveName` when the daemon was not given its name by a launcher,
      `CannotWritePending` when the data directory or the file cannot be written, and
      `CannotReadSource` when the source cannot be read.
  entry .pending   whether a staged candidate is waiting for the launcher
      Whether `.pending` exists in the data directory; false when the directory cannot be
      resolved.
  entry .outcome   what the launcher last did with a staged candidate, until acknowledged
      Reads `.upgrade-result` from the data directory: the result word, the candidate's and
      the previous executable's build ids and when it happened. `Unset` when there is no
      record, it cannot be read, or it names an outcome this version does not know.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.daemonClientEnvironment
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      def report(): Optional[Message] =
        Upgrade.outcome.let: outcome =>
          Upgrade.acknowledge()
          m"${outcome.result} (build ${outcome.candidate.show})"
      ```
  entry .acknowledge   forgets the last outcome, so that an application reports each one once
      Removes `.upgrade-result`; a failure to do so is ignored.
  entry .enabled   whether the running executable's launcher would accept a signed upgrade at all
      The `ethereal.upgradable` property the launcher sets: false for an executable built
      without a release key and application identifier — a development build, whose launcher
      refuses every candidate — and false when the property is absent (an older launcher, or
      no launcher). Needs a `System`.
  entry .Outcome   what the launcher did with a staged candidate: the result, the build ids and when
    detail
        Read back by `Upgrade.outcome` from the record the launcher writes. `candidate` is
        the staged executable's build id (zero if it had no record) and `previous` that of
        the executable which checked it, so a report can say which build replaced which.
    entry .Result   the launcher's verdict on a candidate
      detail
          `Applied` — _the upgrade was applied_; `Disabled` — _the running executable does
          not accept upgrades_ (no key or application id built in); `NoRecord` — _the
          candidate is not an XEK executable_; `WrongApplication` — _the candidate was built
          for another application_, even if signed with the same key; `BadSignature` — _the
          candidate's signature does not verify_, which an unsigned or tampered file gives;
          `NotNewer` — _the candidate is not newer than the running executable_, unless the
          release was signed with `--allow-downgrade`; `SwapFailed` — _the candidate could
          not be swapped into place_. A release signed with the recovery key is applied as
          one signed with the release key is.
      entry .communicable   renders each result as a message
      entry .parse   the result the launcher's word names, or `Unset` for a word this version does not know
          `applied`, `disabled`, `no-record`, `wrong-application`, `bad-signature`,
          `not-newer` and `swap-failed`.
    entry #result   the launcher's verdict
    entry #candidate   the staged executable's build id, or zero if it had no record
    entry #previous   the build id of the executable which checked the candidate
    entry #time   when it happened, in epoch milliseconds
    entry #when   when it happened, as any instant type made from epoch milliseconds
        `outcome.when[Instant]`, for any `Instantiable across Instants from Long`.
  entry .Error   raised when a candidate cannot be staged
    detail
        Error 631: _could not stage the upgrade because `reason`_, raised by `Upgrade.stage`.
        The reason is a `Clarification`, so the full code is `SN-631.1` to `SN-631.3`:
        `CannotReadSource` (1) — _the upgrade source could not be read_; `CannotWritePending`
        (2) — _the .pending file could not be written_; `CannotResolveName` (3) — _the
        running application's name is not available_, which is the case for a JVM run
        without a launcher.
    entry .Reason   why the candidate could not be staged
      detail
          `CannotReadSource`, `CannotWritePending` or `CannotResolveName`, numbered 1 to 3;
          the companion's `Communicable` renders each.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #number   the reason's number within error 631
    entry #reason   why the candidate could not be staged

entry .Terminus   what one of the client's standard streams is attached to: a terminal or a pipe
  detail
      `Terminal` or `Pipe`. The daemon cannot tell for itself — its streams are the
      launcher's socket, not the client's terminal — so each of `Resident#cliInput`,
      `Resident#cliOutput` and `Resident#cliError` is whatever the launcher reported in the
      `init` document, and the three genuinely differ: `mytool > file` run from a terminal
      has stdin on the terminal and stdout on a file. A file, a socket or the null device
      counts as `Pipe`. It encodes to and decodes from the lowercase text `terminal` and
      `pipe`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import threads.virtualThreads

      @main
      def mytool(): Unit = cli:
        execute:
          resident.cliOutput match
            case Terminus.Terminal => Out.println(e"$Bold(a terminal)")
            case Terminus.Pipe     => Out.println(t"a pipe")

          Exit.Ok
      ```
  refer ethereal.Resident
  entry .decoder   reads `terminal` or `pipe` (in any case) as a `Terminus`
  entry .encodable   writes a `Terminus` as `terminal` or `pipe`

entry .Tty   the mode asked of the client's terminal: raw, canonical with echo, or canonical without
  detail
      The launcher raw-modes a terminal stdin before it connects, so that individual
      keypresses can be forwarded to an interactive session. A command which wants ordinary
      line input asks, over the session, for `Canonical` — the terminal driver then provides
      echo and line editing itself — or for `Concealed`, canonical mode with nothing echoed,
      which is how a password is read; `Raw` puts it back. `Resident#cooked` and
      `Resident#concealed` make the requests and restore `Raw` afterwards; `#canonical` and
      `#echo` are the two flags of the `Launcher.Message.Mode` document each becomes.
  refer ethereal.Resident
  entry #canonical   whether the mode is cooked: true for `Canonical` and `Concealed`
  entry #echo   whether the terminal echoes what is typed: true only for `Canonical`

entry .Transcoder   re-encodes a Windows console's input from its code page to UTF-8, a character at a time
    A `java.io.InputStream` over another, decoding bytes in the console's input code page
    (which the `init` document reports as `inputCodepage`, when it is not 65001, UTF-8) and
    delivering their UTF-8 encoding, so that the rest of the daemon reads the invocation's
    input as UTF-8. It reads one character at a time — a surrogate pair as one — so that no
    more of the client's input is taken from the connection than the invocation asked for.
    `cli` wraps the session's stdin in one when the code page calls for it; `available()`
    is the pending bytes of the last character, and `close()` closes the underlying reader.

entry .run   the demo entry point: a daemon which prints _Hello world_
    In `ethereal.example`: the `cli` application the tutorial begins with, in full — the
    four named givens (`backstops.stackTraceBackstop`, `executives.completionsExecutive`,
    `interpreters.posixInterpreter`, `threads.virtualThreads`) and a body which prints
    `Hello world` and exits with `Exit.Ok`. Built with `xek build`, its first invocation
    starts the daemon and every later one returns at once.

entry .fixture   the test fixture entry point: a daemon whose first argument chooses what it exercises
    In `ethereal.example`: the `cli` application the test suite builds into a launcher and
    drives through a shell. Its first argument selects a behaviour — `args` and `lines`
    echo the rest, `echo` and `stderr` write to a stream, `exit` fails with a status,
    `sleep` waits, `env` prints a variable of the invocation's environment, `pid` the
    daemon's pid, `pwd` the invocation's working directory, `cat` and `cooked` read a line
    of stdin raw or in a cooked block, `version` the build id, and the `signal` and
    `trap-…` forms install traps which report what reaches them — and anything else fails
    with status 1. It is a working catalogue of the invocation-side API.
